Task 2: Contrast Non-Idempotent and Idempotent Design
Step 2.1 â Implement and observe a non-idempotent playbook
Design a playbook at playbooks/non_idempotent.yml that contains at least three structurally non-idempotent tasks. Each task must use a shell or command module in a way that produces a new side effect on every invocation regardless of prior state. Concrete requirements:

One task must append a line containing the current timestamp to a file, so the file grows with each run
One task must create a directory whose name includes a runtime-generated value (such as an epoch timestamp), so a new directory appears on every run
One task must use shell to write a key-value pair to a config file without checking whether the pair already exists
Run the playbook twice in succession:

ansible-playbook playbooks/non_idempotent.yml
ansible-playbook playbooks/non_idempotent.yml
After both runs, inspect the filesystem artifacts (the growing file, the duplicate directories, the repeated config lines) and record the changed= count from each run's summary line. Both runs will report non-zero changes. This is the baseline you are fixing in Step 2.2.

Step 2.2 â Rewrite every task as a declarative, idempotent equivalent
Create playbooks/idempotent_core.yml that satisfies the same configuration intent as Step 2.1 but uses only declarative Ansible modules. Architectural constraints:

No shell or command module is permitted unless accompanied by both changed_when: false (for read-only queries) or a precise changed_when expression tied to the command's stdout
Every file-write task must use copy, template, or lineinfile/blockinfile with explicit state, owner, group, and mode parameters
Every service-affecting action must be expressed as a handler triggered by notify, never as an unconditional task
The contract your playbook must satisfy is expressed by the following shell interface â your playbook must make all assertions pass:

# idempotence_contract.sh
# Run this after executing idempotent_core.yml at least twice.
# Every assertion must exit 0.

assert_file_line_count() {
  local file="$1"
  local expected_max="$2"
  local actual
  actual=$(wc -l < "$file")
  [ "$actual" -le "$expected_max" ] \
    || { echo "FAIL: $file has $actual lines, expected <= $expected_max"; exit 1; }
  echo "PASS: $file line count ($actual) within limit"
}

assert_dir_count() {
  local pattern="$1"
  local expected_max="$2"
  local actual
  actual=$(find /tmp -maxdepth 1 -name "$pattern" -type d 2>/dev/null | wc -l)
  [ "$actual" -le "$expected_max" ] \
    || { echo "FAIL: found $actual dirs matching $pattern, expected <= $expected_max"; exit 1; }
  echo "PASS: dir count for $pattern ($actual) within limit"
}

assert_key_unique() {
  local file="$1"
  local key="$2"
  local count
  count=$(grep -c "^${key}=" "$file" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
  [ "$count" -le 1 ] \
    || { echo "FAIL: key $key appears $count times in $file"; exit 1; }
  echo "PASS: key $key appears at most once in $file"
}
Run idempotent_core.yml three times. The second and third runs must report changed=0 in the play recap. If any run after the first reports changes, the playbook is not idempotent â diagnose and fix before continuing.

Step 2.3 â Instrument changed-task counting across runs
Write a Bash test harness at tests/run_convergence_check.sh. The harness must:

Accept a playbook path as its first positional argument
Execute the playbook exactly three times, capturing the full stdout of each run to a separate log file (run_1.log, run_2.log, run_3.log) inside a timestamped results directory under /tmp/
Parse the changed=N value from each run's recap line using only standard POSIX tools (grep, awk, or sed â no Python, no jq)
Exit with code 0 only if runs 2 and 3 both report changed=0; otherwise print which run failed and exit with code 1
The harness interface contract:

# Signature
# Usage: ./tests/run_convergence_check.sh <playbook_path>
# Exit codes:
#   0  â playbook is idempotent (runs 2 and 3 changed=0)
#   1  â playbook is NOT idempotent (at least one post-first run had changes)
#   2  â playbook execution itself failed (non-zero ansible-playbook exit)

run_convergence_check() {
  local playbook_path="$1"   # absolute or relative path to .yml
  local results_dir          # set internally to /tmp/convergence_<epoch>/
  local changed_counts       # array of integers, one per run
  # implementation owned by student
}
Validate the harness against both playbooks:

chmod +x tests/run_convergence_check.sh
./tests/run_convergence_check.sh playbooks/non_idempotent.yml   # must exit 1
./tests/run_convergence_check.sh playbooks/idempotent_core.yml  # must exit 0
Task 3: Production-Grade Idempotent System Configuration
Step 3.1 â Build a multi-role idempotent system playbook
Design playbooks/system_baseline.yml that configures a realistic application baseline on the local machine. The playbook must satisfy all of the following requirements simultaneously:

Create a dedicated system user and group (appuser / appgroup) with a fixed UID/GID (choose values above 2000 to avoid conflicts), a non-login shell, and no home directory creation
Install exactly the packages nginx, git, curl, and tree using the apt module with cache_valid_time set to prevent redundant index fetches on repeated runs
Deploy a templated nginx virtual host configuration to /etc/nginx/sites-available/appsite and symlink it into sites-enabled/; the template must render the server's hostname dynamically using an Ansible fact
Manage the nginx service state through handlers only â no direct state: restarted tasks outside of handlers
Write an application config file to /etc/appsite/app.conf using template or copy with a checksum-stable content block; the file must be owned by appuser:appgroup with mode 0640
Use blockinfile with a unique marker to insert a monitoring stub into /etc/nginx/nginx.conf so repeated runs do not duplicate the block
Architectural constraint: the playbook must be fully re-entrant â running it against a machine that already has nginx installed and running must not cause a service interruption unless the configuration actually changed.

