Step 3.2 â Build a self-validating post-run assertion playbook
Create playbooks/assert_baseline.yml â a standalone playbook that runs after system_baseline.yml and asserts the expected end state without making any changes. Every task in this playbook must have changed_when: false. Requirements:

Assert that appuser exists with the correct UID using the getent command module
Assert that all four required packages report installed state via the dpkg command module
Assert that /etc/appsite/app.conf exists, is owned by appuser:appgroup, and has octal permissions 0640 using the stat module and assert module
Assert that nginx is active and enabled using the systemd module in query mode
Assert that http://localhost returns HTTP 200 using the uri module
Run the full sequence:

ansible-playbook playbooks/system_baseline.yml --diff
ansible-playbook playbooks/system_baseline.yml --diff   # must show changed=0
ansible-playbook playbooks/assert_baseline.yml          # must show ok=N, changed=0, failed=0
./tests/run_convergence_check.sh playbooks/system_baseline.yml  # must exit 0
The assertion playbook interface contract (students implement all tasks):

# assert_baseline.yml structural contract
# Every task must include:
#   changed_when: false
# No task may use shell or command to modify state.
# Allowed modules: stat, assert, command (read-only), uri, systemd, getent (via command), debug
# The play recap must show failed=0 and changed=0 on every run.
Expected Outcomes
The convergence check harness exits 0 for idempotent_core.yml and system_baseline.yml, and exits 1 for non_idempotent.yml, demonstrating measurable, automated idempotence verification
The assertion playbook assert_baseline.yml reports failed=0 and changed=0 on every execution, confirming that the declared end state matches the actual system state
Troubleshooting
If your convergence harness reports changed=N on run 2 for a playbook you believe is idempotent, how would you isolate which specific task is responsible, and what Ansible flags or output modes would give you task-level change detail rather than only the play recap summary?
If assert_baseline.yml fails on the uri module assertion for http://localhost even though systemctl is-active nginx returns active, what are the three most likely configuration-level causes, and how would you distinguish between them using only tools available on the instance?
Conclusion
Idempotence is not a property of individual modules in isolation â it emerges from the combination of module choice, parameter completeness, handler discipline, and the absence of runtime-generated side effects in task definitions. The convergence harness you built externalizes this property into a measurable, automatable signal rather than a manual inspection. Applying the assert playbook pattern as a post-run gate transforms idempotence from a design aspiration into an enforceable contract that can be integrated into any CI pipeline.
